    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, can u help me make sense of this clause in an NDA? i'm a dev and i want to make sure i'm not gonna get sued if i use similar logic in my next gig. here is the text:\n\n\"Notwithstanding the foregoing, nothing in this Agreement shall restrict the Receiving Party from using the Residuals (defined as ideas, concepts, know-how, or techniques retained in the unaided memory of the Receiving Party's employees) for any purpose, provided that the Receiving Party does not disclose any Confidential Information in violation of this Agreement.\"\n\ndoes this mean i can still use general coding patterns or architectural stuff i learn while working for them?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, this clause is designed to protect you from being sued for using general knowledge you pick up while working with the company. It essentially means that the company can't own the *ideas* or *techniques* that stick in your head after you've seen them, as long as you don't leak their actual secrets. Think of it like this: you can't steal the blueprint of their specific, secret weapon (that's the Confidential Information), but you *can* use the general knowledge of how to build a decent catapult (that's the Residuals). You are safe using general coding patterns or architectural concepts you learn.",
